    "chunk_size_bytes": 1767,
    "chunk_text": "Journey\u2019s end: Reflecting, speaking, and writing on Martin Wickramasinghe\n\n\u201cWe only see what we look at. To look is an act of choice. \u201c\n\nOn Tuesday, 31 March 2026, the Martin Wickramasinghe Trust will hold a special lecture on the novelist, critic, journalist, and thinker Martin Wickramasinghe in the Garden Gallery at Sapumal Foundation. Moderated by Samindi Warnasooriya from Open University, with opening remarks by Avishka Mario Senewiratne and closing remarks by Hasini Haputhanthri, it will focus on Wickramasinghe\u2019s views on art and art history.\n\nThis is the seventh in a series of lectures undertaken by the Trust. It is also the last. The first of them was a webinar which was organised by the School of Oriental and African Studies (SOAS) in the UK. The second was a presentation at the India International Centre in New Delhi. Both took place within a week of each other: 13 and 20 November. The following week, on 27 November, the night Cyclone Ditwah struck Sri Lanka, the Trust held the first of its lectures in Sri Lanka at Lakmahal Community Library.\n\nEach of these presentations has focused on specific aspects to Wickramasinghe, his life, work, and thought. The SOAS webinar was a general lecture, framing Wickramasinghe as a Sri Lankan and South Asian mind. The New Delhi lecture dissected his writings against the backdrop of modernism in Sri Lanka and India. The Lakmahal presentation focused on the books and authors he read when he was in Colombo from 1906 to 1916 and how these influenced him. Lectures at the Social Scientists\u2019 Association, on 10 December, and the International Centre for Ethnic Studies (ICES) Colombo, on 6 February 2026, framed him as a social scientist, with the SSA lecture focusing on his political writings."
  },
