    "md5": "e451074b4f2cdcc1e1a91724dc060b0b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1984,
    "chunk_text": "Each of these presentations has focused on specific aspects to Wickramasinghe, his life, work, and thought. The SOAS webinar was a general lecture, framing Wickramasinghe as a Sri Lankan and South Asian mind. The New Delhi lecture dissected his writings against the backdrop of modernism in Sri Lanka and India. The Lakmahal presentation focused on the books and authors he read when he was in Colombo from 1906 to 1916 and how these influenced him. Lectures at the Social Scientists\u2019 Association, on 10 December, and the International Centre for Ethnic Studies (ICES) Colombo, on 6 February 2026, framed him as a social scientist, with the SSA lecture focusing on his political writings.\n\nPlacing him against the social and economic conditions of his time, the Marga lecture went on to examine his encounters with radical politics and capitalism in Colombo, at first as a shop assistant and later an employee of the formidable D. R. Wijewardene. The relationship between Wickramasinghe and Wijewardene, in particular, has been written about many times. Yet we know little about how it moulded his career. It is clear that through D. R. Wijewardene and his colleagues, Wickramasinghe accessed a world he had not been privy to until then. The lecture explained how this shaped his writings.\n\nFor someone who wrote so extensively on such a wide range of topics, it is astonishing how Wickramasinghe has been restricted today to a handful of novels and other works. Most Sri Lankans, especially Sinhala-speakers, identify him as the author of Madol Duwa, the Koggala Trilogy, and Viragaya. Even if they have not read these texts, it is as their author that Wickramasinghe is celebrated and venerated today, by scholars, students, and every other Sri Lankan. Part of the rationale for these lectures has been to compel audiences to think differently about him. They were timed for his 135th birth anniversary, which fell last year, and his 50th death anniversary, which falls this July."
